A test that is not connected to one of its services is `Skipped` with reason `ServiceNotConnected`.
`ActiveDirectory` is opt-in: its tests are `NotRun` with reason `OptInServiceNotConnected` until you connect it.
A custom test that names a service the registry does not know is `Skipped` with reason `ServiceNotRegistered`.

### License

`License` lists Microsoft **service plan** names: the `servicePlanName` values of the tenant's `subscribedSkus`.
The tenant needs any one element; inside an element, `&` means all of the joined plans.

Use the service plan, not the product. Microsoft lists both in
[Product names and service plan identifiers for licensing](https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/identity/users/licensing-service-plan-reference):
take the name from the **Service plans included** column, such as `AAD_PREMIUM_P2`. The **Product name** and
**String ID** columns, such as Microsoft 365 E5 and `SPE_E5`, are products. A product is a bundle that Microsoft
re-packages over time, and the same feature ships in many of them, so a test that named a product would be
skipped in tenants that have the feature through another one. The [test builder](/test-builder) has a
licence explorer that finds the service plan from a product name, a friendly name or a GUID.

```powershell
License = 'AAD_PREMIUM_P2'                        # Entra ID P2
License = ('AAD_PREMIUM', 'INTUNE_A')             # Entra ID P1 (or better) or Intune
License = 'AAD_PREMIUM_P2&INTUNE_A'               # Entra ID P2 and Intune
```

Maester maps these tokens to the service plan and SKU IDs it has always used to detect each product, so
government, education and suite variants match too:

| Token | Tenant has |
| --- | --- |
| `AAD_PREMIUM` | Entra ID P1 or better (P1, P2 or ID Governance) |
| `AAD_PREMIUM_P2` | Entra ID P2 |
| `Entra_Identity_Governance` | Microsoft Entra ID Governance |
| `AAD_WRKLDID_P1`, `AAD_WRKLDID_P2` | Workload ID P1 or better, Workload ID P2 |
